{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 26, :comments ["<b>What gain is it that we kill our brother and cover his blood.</b> In every place where the word בֶּצַע appears in Scripture, it speaks of monetary benefit — and here, from where would monetary benefit come to them through killing him? Therefore it appears to me that the entire root of the jealousy was over the birthright, for the brothers perceived through the striped tunic that he would give him the birthright, as explained above (Genesis 37:3). And since the law of the firstborn is to take a double portion, therefore they went to graze the flock of their father near Shechem — a place prepared for calamities and danger from the surrounding nations of Shechem, lest they plunder their sheep and cattle, because the sons of Jacob had taken their sheep and cattle when they came upon Shechem. And the brothers were not concerned even if enemies should rise against them and take the entire flock, saying: 'If the youngest among us takes a double portion, then neither for us nor for him will there be anything' — and they despaired, each one willing to lose his own share so long as Joseph would lose two shares, like the tale of the envious man and the covetous man who came before Solomon. And when they went to Shechem, Jacob perceived their scheme and sent Joseph, saying, 'Go see the welfare of your brothers and the welfare of the flock' (Genesis 37:14) — from the enemies surrounding them. And Joseph said, 'It is my brothers I seek' (Genesis 37:16), and I am not concerned about the flock. Therefore he said, 'Are not your brothers grazing בִּשְׁכֶם?' (Genesis 37:13), and the expression 'הֲלֹא' conveys thus: Is not this place dangerous and prepared for calamities? And he did not say 'grazing the flock' — we may say that they were grazing for themselves and were unconcerned about the flock, for the reason explained above." "And what they said — <b>Come, let us kill him</b> — it appears to explain that they considered him a talebearer, and every talebearer causes bloodshed, as it is written (Ezekiel 22:9), 'Talebearers were in you for the purpose of shedding blood.' And our Rabbis of blessed memory further said (Deuteronomy Rabbah 5:10), 'Evil tongue kills three' and so forth. Therefore it is stated, 'And before he drew near to them, they conspired against him to put him to death' (Genesis 37:18) — for one who comes to kill you, rise early to kill him; this is what it says: before he drew near to them to kill them, they conspired to put him to death beforehand — before he drew near to them. This is what it says, 'And now, come, let us kill him' (Genesis 37:20) — what is 'and now'? Rather, now, immediately, before he draws near to us — and this is a precious interpretation. And above we explained this verse in another way, at the verse 'And Joseph brought an evil report of them' (Genesis 37:2). 'And Reuben heard and delivered him from their hand' (Genesis 37:21) — for he was afraid lest they pin all the blame on him, so that the birthright that had been taken from him and given to Joseph would revert to him." "<b>And Judah said: What gain.</b> Of money will we have, that we kill our brother and cover his blood? If we had permission to publicize the matter, there would indeed be monetary gain here — for once the birthright was taken from Reuben, if so Reuben the firstborn is removed, and Joseph is dead, then the portion of the firstborn would revert to all the brothers equally. But now, because of our father's grief we have no permission to publicize the matter, and all the days it will remain in doubt — perhaps Joseph is alive and lost, or sold to a distant land; if so, even after our father's death his portion will remain held in court until it becomes known what happened to him, and we would need to bring proof that he was killed — because we already said before our father that he was not killed. If so there is no monetary gain here. 'Come, let us sell him to the Ishmaelites' — then there will be monetary gain for us, for there he will be a permanent slave, and if so, whatever a slave acquires, his master acquires, and assuredly our father will remove his inheritance entirely, for what benefit would it be to him if he bequeaths to him and his master comes and takes all that is his." "And regarding the sale to the Ishmaelites and the Midianites, it appears that this is its explanation: the brothers wished to sell him only to the Ishmaelites alone, for they said that Ishmael is the brother of Isaac, and from the side of kinship he will have compassion on him. And perhaps it was regarding Ishmael that they said, 'For he is our brother, our flesh' (Genesis 37:27), in the sense of 'for we are kinsmen' (Genesis 13:8). But these Ishmaelites did not wish to buy him, for they were not traders, and as long as they had no trader who would come back and buy from them immediately, hand to hand, they did not wish to buy him. And when afterward Midianite men, traders, passed by, then the Ishmaelites saw that there were traders who would come back and buy from them, then they agreed to buy. This is what is written: 'And they drew and lifted Joseph from the pit, and they sold Joseph to the Ishmaelites' (Genesis 37:28) — and by implication we hear that the Ishmaelites sold him to the Midianites without the brothers' consent, or without their knowledge, and the Midianites in turn sold him to Potiphar, etc. And when Reuben returned to the pit and the boy was gone, he said, 'And I, where can I go?' (Genesis 37:30) — for if on account of the birthright portion you killed Joseph, then perhaps you will do the same to me so that the birthright will not revert to me; 'And I, where can I go' — for where can I flee from you. And it may even be that because of this fear, Reuben wished to save him from the outset."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 37, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:32:36.639280945Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}